WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.750
The bottom track is NCBI RefSeq annotating the same region.

2
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:11.750
Keeping it separate from GENCODE is deliberate — you can always see which collection an annotation came from.

3
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:19.750
And because both are drawn on the same coordinates, you can compare them directly: where they agree on an exon boundary, and where they do not.

4
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:27.075
This subtrack is the curated part of RefSeq: reference messenger RNAs, and non-coding RNA records.

5
00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:31.250
The computationally predicted ones are in a separate subtrack.

6
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:40.675
So this is the reviewed material, which is a sensible place to start, although each record still has its own evidence and review status worth checking.

7
00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:47.450
The small arrows along the HBB model point left, while the coordinates increase to the right.

8
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:53.200
HBB is on the minus strand, so transcription runs towards lower coordinates.

9
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.400
In this picture the five-prime end of the transcript is on the right and the three-prime end is on the left.

10
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.400
The green RNA below points the other way, because it is on the other strand.

11
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.625
This matters as soon as you retrieve a sequence.

12
00:01:07.625 --> 00:01:14.025
Ask for it in the gene's orientation and you get something different from the chromosome's forward strand.

13
00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:18.925
Zoomed in on the rightmost exon, where this transcript begins.

14
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:22.975
The thin part is the five-prime untranslated region.

15
00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:30.850
Where it thickens, coding starts, and the green mark at that boundary is the start codon — the methionine the protein begins with.

16
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:38.525
Inside the coding part, alternating shades of blue separate one codon from the next, so the reading frame is visible.

17
00:01:38.525 --> 00:01:43.200
Green marks methionine wherever it occurs, not only at the beginning.

18
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.550
It is the position, at the boundary where coding starts, that makes this one the start.

19
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:55.075
Following the transcript left brings us to the last exon, and a red mark: the stop codon.

20
00:01:55.075 --> 00:01:57.625
The RNA does not end there.

21
00:01:57.625 --> 00:02:03.200
It carries on into the three-prime untranslated region, the thinner stretch to the left.

22
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.125
The stop codon ends translation, not the transcript.

23
00:02:07.125 --> 00:02:13.100
And note that this small red mark means something entirely different from the large red feature earlier.

24
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.175
Same colour, different track, different meaning.

25
00:02:16.775 --> 00:02:20.700
Which brings us to a question that sounds simple and is not.

26
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.175
Someone asks you for "the HBB sequence".

27
00:02:24.175 --> 00:02:25.700
Which one?

28
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.800
The DNA at its place on the chromosome?

29
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.075
The mature transcript?

30
00:02:31.075 --> 00:02:33.950
Only the part that codes for protein?

31
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.075
The protein itself?

32
00:02:36.075 --> 00:02:48.550
We will take them one at a time, all from a single annotated transcript — the reviewed human RefSeq transcript, on GRCh38 — so that every difference in length has a reason you can point at.

33
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:56.675
And notice already that this transcript spans sixteen hundred and eight bases, while the window we have been looking at is wider than that.

34
00:02:56.675 --> 00:03:00.250
The window and the transcript are not the same boundary.

35
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.850
Two answers come straight off the record.

36
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:11.475
The transcript is six hundred and twenty-eight nucleotides — its three exons joined, untranslated regions included.

37
00:03:11.475 --> 00:03:15.575
The protein is a hundred and forty-seven amino acids.

38
00:03:15.575 --> 00:03:21.200
The two are connected, but the transcript plainly contains more than the protein accounts for.

39
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.475
One thing to notice: the transcript is written with T, not U.

40
00:03:25.475 --> 00:03:29.325
That is a storage convention and nothing biological.

41
00:03:29.325 --> 00:03:35.700
You cannot tell from the letters alone whether you are holding a transcript or a piece of genomic DNA.

42
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.300
Only the record tells you that.

43
00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:51.975
For the genomic version you have to say what to include, and the export menu is a list of biological choices: the five-prime untranslated region, the three-prime untranslated region, coding exons, introns.

44
00:03:51.975 --> 00:03:56.600
Take all of them and you get the complete genomic span of this transcript.

45
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:05.025
Add upstream or downstream bases and you have gone past the transcript into the DNA around it, and the length changes accordingly.

46
00:04:05.025 --> 00:04:10.650
The formatting options are separate, and they change presentation rather than content.

47
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:19.150
Exons in capitals and introns in lower case is a good habit — it makes the organisation legible without altering a single base.

48
00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.550
Sixteen hundred and eight nucleotides, because the introns are still in.

49
00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:30.050
The two introns contribute a hundred and thirty bases and eight hundred and fifty.

50
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:36.350
Take those nine hundred and eighty away and six hundred and twenty-eight are left, which is the transcript.

51
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.950
The arithmetic follows from the exon structure and from nothing else.

52
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:50.825
The capitals cover the untranslated parts of the exons as well as the coding parts — a useful reminder that an exon and a coding region are not the same thing.

53
00:04:50.825 --> 00:04:58.225
And because HBB is on the minus strand, what comes back is the reverse complement of the chromosome's forward sequence.

54
00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:03.875
That is the gene's orientation, so this and the transcript can be read side by side.

55
00:05:04.475 --> 00:05:10.075
Take the untranslated regions out and four hundred and forty-four nucleotides remain.

56
00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:15.625
Fifty of them were in front, at the five-prime end, and a hundred and thirty-four behind.

57
00:05:15.625 --> 00:05:19.200
It starts with ATG and ends with TAA.

58
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.800
A hundred and forty-eight triplets: a hundred and forty-seven codons for amino acids, and one stop.

59
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.525
Which is exactly the protein we were handed earlier, initiator methionine included.

60
00:05:31.525 --> 00:05:34.750
The stop codon contributes no residue.

61
00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.875
One trap in the header.

62
00:05:36.875 --> 00:05:42.525
The coordinates there are the outer boundaries of the coding region, and they span the introns.

63
00:05:42.525 --> 00:05:47.625
Subtract them and you have measured a piece of chromosome, not the length of this sequence.

64
00:05:48.225 --> 00:05:50.875
So the four versions fit together.

65
00:05:50.875 --> 00:05:55.200
Genomic DNA keeps the exon and intron organisation.

66
00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.700
The transcript is the exons joined, with both untranslated regions still there.

67
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.425
The coding sequence is the part that gets translated.

68
00:06:04.425 --> 00:06:07.000
The protein is what comes out.

69
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.775
Two of those arrows are things the cell does — transcription, then splicing.

70
00:06:11.775 --> 00:06:13.750
The third is not.

71
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:18.450
Choosing the coding sequence is a decision you make about what to analyse.

72
00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:23.100
Which is why the shortest sequence is not automatically the most useful.

73
00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.900
A variant in an intron needs the genomic sequence.

74
00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.975
A question about an untranslated region needs something that still has one.

75
00:06:31.975 --> 00:06:37.000
A question about the amino acids needs the coding sequence, or the protein.

76
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.925
Work out what you are asking before you decide what to download.

77
00:06:41.525 --> 00:06:46.125
We have pulled DNA, RNA and protein out of one transcript.

78
00:06:46.125 --> 00:06:48.650
Now a different kind of question.

79
00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:52.775
Suppose you want to find a DNA variant and see where it falls.

80
00:06:52.775 --> 00:06:57.100
Look at this display and ask which parts of it would actually help you.

81
00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:04.375
Both annotation sources are on screen, GENCODE and RefSeq, drawn against the same coordinates.

82
00:07:04.375 --> 00:07:12.500
We will keep the RefSeq transcript we have been using, so variant positions can be compared against exons and introns we already know.

83
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.225
Zoomed in on HBB, RefSeq only.

84
00:07:16.225 --> 00:07:20.350
Thick blocks are coding, the thin line between them is an intron.

85
00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:24.200
That is our reference for reading anything we add on top.

86
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.950
Underneath is where the rest of the browser lives, in collapsed categories.

87
00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:32.325
Expression, if we wanted RNA abundance.

88
00:07:32.325 --> 00:07:35.875
Comparative genomics, regulation, repeats.

89
00:07:35.875 --> 00:07:43.375
Our question is about variants with clinical interpretations attached, so we open phenotypes, variants and literature.

90
00:07:43.975 --> 00:07:48.400
We add the ClinVar track and keep only its short-variant subtrack.

91
00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.700
The parent track is set to pack and the subtrack to dense, which collapses it into one compact row.

92
00:07:54.700 --> 00:08:03.725
That is what we want — a single row of variant marks sitting directly under the transcript, so you can look straight up from a mark and see what it lands on.

93
00:08:04.325 --> 00:08:07.875
Before asking what a variant does, ask where it is.

94
00:08:07.875 --> 00:08:12.900
Compare each mark in the ClinVar row with the transcript directly above it.

95
00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.425
The first arrow points at a mark over the thin line.

96
00:08:16.425 --> 00:08:18.525
That is the intron.

97
00:08:18.525 --> 00:08:21.675
The second points at one over a thick block.

98
00:08:21.675 --> 00:08:25.075
That is coding, where a change can alter a codon.

99
00:08:25.075 --> 00:08:28.100
The coding one is easy to reason about.

100
00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:34.250
So what could the intronic one possibly do, given that the intron is cut out of the RNA anyway?

101
00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.275
Take the coding one first.

102
00:08:37.275 --> 00:08:41.175
Zoomed to individual bases, an A becomes a T.

103
00:08:41.175 --> 00:08:47.400
GAG becomes GTG, and glutamate at position seven of beta-globin becomes valine.

104
00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.500
Haemoglobin carrying that beta-globin is haemoglobin S.

105
00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:57.100
Now the intronic one — twenty-one bases before the start of exon two, a G becoming an A.

106
00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:00.850
That creates a new site the cell can use when it splices.

107
00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:09.925
And when the cell uses it, the last nineteen bases of the first intron stay in the mature messenger RNA, bringing a premature stop codon with them.

108
00:09:09.925 --> 00:09:16.900
Some transcripts from that copy still splice normally, so the result is less working beta-globin rather than none.

109
00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:24.050
Which answers the question: a change inside an intron can matter a great deal, by changing where the intron ends.

110
00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.875
The browser told us where.

111
00:09:26.875 --> 00:09:30.350
It does not tell us how any of that was established.

112
00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.925
For that you follow the link back.

113
00:09:32.925 --> 00:09:40.450
Opening the intronic variant's details at UCSC gives an accession, and the accession leads to the ClinVar record.

114
00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:47.300
There you get the exact change, the condition it was assessed against, the individual submissions, and the papers cited.

115
00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:54.250
In this case somebody looked at the RNA directly and found the extra intronic sequence we have just been discussing.

116
00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:57.175
That is the habit worth taking away.

117
00:09:57.175 --> 00:10:01.725
The browser is the map; the record underneath it is the evidence.

118
00:10:01.725 --> 00:10:04.775
Now the same region in a different browser.

119
00:10:05.375 --> 00:10:12.700
Ensembl organises the same kinds of information differently, and it is worth being able to find your bearings in both.

120
00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:16.600
Top label: the assembly, GRCh38 again.

121
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.825
Then the coordinates, the stretch of chromosome eleven on screen.

122
00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:29.700
Then HBB itself — blocks for exons, lines for introns, and small chevrons pointing left, because the gene is on the reverse strand.

123
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:34.600
Below it the non-coding RNA, pointing the other way, on the other strand.

124
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.550
And a possible transcript at the bottom.

125
00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:41.025
Everything we learned to read at UCSC is here.

126
00:10:41.025 --> 00:10:43.550
Only the layout has changed.

127
00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:52.475
Ensembl's Feature Explorer puts the download options in one place, and it makes the point of the last ten minutes better than anything else could.

128
00:10:52.475 --> 00:10:57.625
The menu has two columns, one for the gene and one for the selected transcript.

129
00:10:57.625 --> 00:11:04.750
Check which column you are in before you click, because both of them offer genomic sequence and they do not mean the same thing.

130
00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:09.950
The whole annotated gene is three thousand nine hundred and thirty-two bases.

131
00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:14.600
The genomic region of this one transcript is sixteen hundred and eight.

132
00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:21.225
Its exons are a hundred and forty-two, two hundred and twenty-three, and two hundred and sixty-three bases.

133
00:11:21.225 --> 00:11:24.500
Spliced together, six hundred and twenty-eight.

134
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.800
Coding sequence, four hundred and forty-four.

135
00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.700
Protein, a hundred and forty-seven amino acids.

136
00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:39.125
Six different answers to "send me the HBB sequence", every one of them correct, all from a single gene record.

137
00:11:39.125 --> 00:11:41.575
So say which one you want.

